  "question": {
    "text": "<p>An ideal gas enclosed in a vertical cylindrical container supports a freely moving piston of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">M</mtext></math>. The piston and the cylinder have equal cross-sectional area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">A</mtext></math>. When the piston is in equilibrium, the volume of the gas is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> and its pressure is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. The piston is slightly displaced from the equilibrium position and released. Assuming that the system is completely isolated from its surrounding, the piston executes a simple harmonic motion with frequency<br />[Assume the system is in space.]</p>"
